  "query_preview": "..\nThrough Sunday...\n\nTemperatures have warmed into the 70s most places this afternoon,\nwith breezy northwest winds over the east (20-40 mph gusts).\nHeading into tonight, a jet max moving overhead and weak warm air\nadvection may bring isolated to widely scattered showers (15-25%)\nto northeastern areas, mainly after midnight into the morning \nhours.\n\nWarm, dry and breezy conditions can be expected for Tuesday, ahead\nof a stronger shortwave and associated cold that will drop into\nthe region Tuesday night/Wednesday. Winds increase during the\nmorning Tuesday, with westerly winds gusting to 40-55 mph over the\nwestern foothills during the afternoon, with gradually lower \nwinds to the east. Temperatures will warm into the 80s with some\nareas approaching 90 degrees. While the recent rainfall and green\nup should prevent widespread fire weather concerns, there are\nareas that have seen less precipitation, leading to local elevated\nfire weather concerns. Take care not to cause a spark.\n\nAs the aforementioned wave and associated cold front drop through\nthe region Tuesday night/Wednesday, breezy northwest winds are \nexpected to spread over the entire area through Wednesday. \nWidespread wind gusts of 25 to 40 mph are expected, with the \nstrongest winds over the far east and down along the east side of \nthe Bighorns where gusts of 40-50 mph are possible. Shower and \nthunderstorm chances increase behind the front for northern and \neastern areas into Wednesday as well (15-50% chance). Increased \ninstability and shear may bring a few stronger storms to northeast\nMT, while weaker thunderstorms are expected down into southeast \nMT at this time. SPC currently has a marginal risk for severe\nthunderstorms over NE MT into the Dakotas. Temperatures will be a\ngood 10-20 degrees cooler on Wednesday with highs in the 60s and \n70s.\n\nNorthwest flow looks to remain over the area through Friday, with\nweak waves producing isolated showers/thunderstorms (20% chance)\nat times, mainly along our periphery. Heading into the weekend,\nensembles continue to prog southwest flow developing over the \nnorthern Rockies as upper troughing moves into the West. This \nwould bring a better potential for daily thunderstorms over the \nupcoming weekend with shortwaves lifting into the region and \nplains moisture advecting into the area. Temperatures are forecast\nin the 70s most days Thursday through Sunday. STP",
